Database migrations (Alembic)
=============================

The database URL comes from `backend.config.settings.DATABASE_URL` via env.py,
not from alembic.ini, so migrations always target the same database the app
runs against.

Common commands (run from the repository root):

    alembic current                     # which revision is applied
    alembic history                     # all revisions
    alembic upgrade head                # apply outstanding migrations
    alembic downgrade -1                # roll back one revision
    alembic check                       # fail if models have drifted

To add a migration after changing a model in backend/database.py:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"

Always read the generated script before applying it. Autogenerate detects
schema shape, not intent — it cannot know that a rename is a rename rather
than a drop plus an add, and it will not migrate data for you.


SQLite: batch mode and foreign keys
-----------------------------------

SQLite cannot ALTER most constraints in place, so env.py sets
`render_as_batch=True`. Batch mode rebuilds the table: create a new one, copy
rows, drop the original, rename.

That interacts badly with `PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON`, which backend.database
enables on every connection. Dropping a table makes any ON DELETE CASCADE
pointing at it fire, deleting child rows — rebuilding `tg_bot_configs` will
silently empty `tg_channels`. env.py therefore disables enforcement for the
duration of the migration and runs `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` afterwards,
raising if the rebuild left anything dangling.

Back up the database before applying a migration that rebuilds a table with
data in it, and check row counts afterwards.


Revisions
---------

44a67695df92  baseline schema
              The full schema as it stood when Alembic was introduced.
              Pre-existing databases were stamped with this rather than
              having it applied.

3a9fabb7d6cf  enforce workspace foreign keys and token hash index
              The workspace_id columns had been added by the hand-rolled
              ensure_saas_columns() helper as plain INTEGERs with no foreign
              key, so the ON DELETE CASCADE declared on the models was never
              enforced and deleting a workspace orphaned its tenant data.
              Also adds the missing index on tg_bot_configs.bot_token_hash.
